  "question": {
    "text": "Consider two reactions having same Arrhenius factor A, but different energy of activation.<br />\r(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20&nbsp;</mn><mi>kJ</mi></math><br />\r(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mo>;</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30&nbsp;</mn><mi>kJ</mi></math><br />\rBoth are at temperature <math><mn>25</mn><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math><br />\rIf temperature in both reaction is increased slightly in such a way that change in temperature in both case is same than choose the correct options.",
    "image": null
  },
